WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:02.959
Film Courage: What have you found is the best
way to make money as a filmmaker?

00:00:02.959 --> 00:00:10.940
Ryan Harriss, Filmmaker: I think that's a
tough question. It's really important that

00:00:10.940 --> 00:00:19.949
obviously a lot of people I think forget that
filmmaking, you have to pay people for what

00:00:19.949 --> 00:00:29.039
they do because there's the thing in Los Angeles
that I’ve noticed a lot is people tend to

00:00:29.039 --> 00:00:35.060
use exposure as currency and I think that
becomes Oh well you're going to get so much

00:00:35.060 --> 00:00:38.610
exposure if you do this for free and for free
and I think a lot of people try and get freebies

00:00:38.610 --> 00:00:43.520
from people but I think making money as a
filmmaker is something that I don't think

00:00:43.520 --> 00:00:55.050
ever gets easier. I always have to take side
jobs, editing jobs or jobs on set and stuff

00:00:55.050 --> 00:01:00.390
like that or for example I worked in a rental
house and whatever you can do that's going

00:01:00.390 --> 00:01:07.800
to pay your bills that gets you the closest
to filmmaking and movie making is just as

00:01:07.800 --> 00:01:08.800
important.

00:01:08.800 --> 00:01:14.300
Film Courage: Without naming names are you
able to share one of your worst jobs? You

00:01:14.300 --> 00:01:20.750
don't have to say of course where. We'd rather
you not but it just what the duties were.

00:01:20.750 --> 00:01:30.280
Ryan: I’ve had a lot of bad jobs I worked
the director I was friends with the actor

00:01:30.280 --> 00:01:36.100
and the the actor knew that I was a director
and the director of that actual project was

00:01:36.100 --> 00:01:43.130
like this tyrannical sort of intense he would
I think he thought that shouting and yelling

00:01:43.130 --> 00:01:49.250
equaled direction and and and he was get we
would get frustrated all the time and we were

00:01:49.250 --> 00:01:58.070
we were traveling all over the country and
there was one experience where he my friend

00:01:58.070 --> 00:02:03.270
who was the actor was trying to perform this
scene and he wasn't quite getting it the way

00:02:03.270 --> 00:02:08.789
that the director wanted it and he came up
to me because he knew I was a director and

00:02:08.789 --> 00:02:12.209
he said what like what are you what do you
think and I was just there as an AC [focus

00:02:12.209 --> 00:02:15.310
puller, or 1st assistant camera] I was there
pulling focus I didn't want to get involved

00:02:15.310 --> 00:02:19.750
in any of that stuff but he was a friend of
mine so I sat there I was like 20 I think

00:02:19.750 --> 00:02:24.400
at the time so I was like well if you maybe
do this or it might be more interesting if

00:02:24.400 --> 00:02:28.920
you try this or that so he would try those
things and the director would just get furious

00:02:28.920 --> 00:02:33.780
at him and was like where are you getting
this who is telling all this information and

00:02:33.780 --> 00:02:36.650
he would point right at me and say oh Ryan
I was talking to Ryan earlier and he would

00:02:36.650 --> 00:02:43.900
tell and I’m just like no so I got yelled
at in a trailer pretty intensely for a while

00:02:43.900 --> 00:02:48.810
about that so there's I mean there's been
some really really bad you work with a lot

00:02:48.810 --> 00:02:58.680
of people that that are that that have egos
that don't seem warranted or necessary or

00:02:58.680 --> 00:03:06.000
even earned to be honest so a lot of the sets
that i've worked on have been are pretty difficult

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:10.360
and and can be very difficult but then you
work on great sets with with amazing people

00:03:10.360 --> 00:03:17.160
and you you earn a lot of amazing friendships
that way too but I i still have some nightmares

00:03:17.160 --> 00:03:24.300
from that do you think that coming from another
state there were some unspoken rules that

00:03:24.300 --> 00:03:29.319
it took a while to learn because I know other
states even Northern California there's a

00:03:29.319 --> 00:03:35.739
different way people approach people and just
learning that that unspoken culture yeah it

00:03:35.739 --> 00:03:39.700
took some time the culture of like filmmaking
of like film sets and stuff like that yeah

00:03:39.700 --> 00:03:44.290
or and and just being in LA and whether to
pick somebody a script that you see it's well

00:03:44.290 --> 00:03:50.301
at a restaurant or yeah I think where I grew
up I was very isolated like I said it was

00:03:50.301 --> 00:03:59.739
a very very small town I had one movie theater
and one video rental spot and so the culture

00:03:59.739 --> 00:04:07.800
in that town was very positive it was very
sort of like you can do anything that you

00:04:07.800 --> 00:04:13.650
want to do and and you can be anything that
you want to be and everybody was very supportive

00:04:13.650 --> 00:04:18.829
so you had this sort of community I think
I was like one out of two people that wanted

00:04:18.829 --> 00:04:23.719
to make movies in that town so there was this
sort of culture of like well you're the one

00:04:23.719 --> 00:04:27.539
guy that wants to do this so everybody kind
of if we do if we need something like that

00:04:27.539 --> 00:04:33.949
done we go to you so it was a lot sort of
more community oriented and friendly and then

00:04:33.949 --> 00:04:39.260
moving out la it's just there's just more
people and there's more money and there's

00:04:39.260 --> 00:04:45.860
more ideas and there's just it's so it's it's
a completely different culture to the pacific

00:04:45.860 --> 00:04:52.139
north northwest as far as just people go i've
never lived in a place with so many people

00:04:52.139 --> 00:04:55.379
that's actually been so isolating it's really
interesting how isolating los Los Angeles

00:04:55.379 --> 00:05:01.509
can be you there's this the glitz and glamour
of Hollywood and you have this 60 mile wide

00:05:01.509 --> 00:05:07.159
landscape of Los Angeles that is actually
really beautiful and you somehow feel just

00:05:07.159 --> 00:05:12.620
so alone when you move out here you feel at
least I did in my experience I felt so well

00:05:12.620 --> 00:05:16.469
i'm by myself and I got to figure out how
to do this and and and make it work so so

00:05:16.469 --> 00:05:21.249
in that regards yeah they're two very different
cultures and it is different culture film

00:05:21.249 --> 00:05:26.279
set wise too I worked in a couple film sets
up in up in Portland [Oregon] and there is

00:05:26.279 --> 00:05:33.289
a there is a sort of a a different vibe being
small fish or I’m sorry being big fish in

00:05:33.289 --> 00:05:40.990
a smaller pond up north tends to drive more
egos I think whereas in my experience down

00:05:40.990 --> 00:05:47.119
here there's a lot of fish but it's a huge
pond and though there are very egotistical

00:05:47.119 --> 00:05:53.749
people there are a lot of really really good
people who just want to continue to do what

00:05:53.749 --> 00:05:59.599
they love so I had a very, very different
experience on film sets down here versus versus

00:05:59.599 --> 00:06:05.869
up there how important is it for you to quit
a day job so that you can put all your time

00:06:05.869 --> 00:06:12.199
into filmmaking or is that not something that's
important to you that's yeah I think it's

00:06:12.199 --> 00:06:21.539
really important I think it's easy to get
it's easy to get comfortable excuse me especially

00:06:21.539 --> 00:06:26.610
in a job like a rental house it's very easy
to sort of sit there and go well i'm getting

00:06:26.610 --> 00:06:31.729
paid I get to work with cameras I get to do
for the most part be around the things that

00:06:31.729 --> 00:06:40.189
I love and meet some really amazing people
but I think it's really really important that

00:06:40.189 --> 00:06:49.460
if you can to just focus on what you love
and really really work really really hard

00:06:49.460 --> 00:06:57.460
at it and put a lot of time into it and I
think a day job is great people you at the

00:06:57.460 --> 00:07:03.270
end of the day we all have to pay bills and
we all we have to live so I don't think there

00:07:03.270 --> 00:07:07.349
should be any level of of pride where you
say well i'm not going to take this job i'm

00:07:07.349 --> 00:07:11.900
better than that or i'm better than this I
think that anything that's going to allow

00:07:11.900 --> 00:07:14.539
you to continue to do what you do.

00:07:14.539 --> 00:07:20.149
Hold on to that because for me it was very
much working at a rental house still allowed

00:07:20.149 --> 00:07:27.479
me to make movies and work on the weekends
and meet people in the film industry and make

00:07:27.479 --> 00:07:34.779
really really great connections with people
but having not worked there now being here

00:07:34.779 --> 00:07:40.499
full time and doing working on my company
full time in our movie full-time it's the

00:07:40.499 --> 00:07:46.889
most rewarding thing that I've ever done in
my life so far so it's definitely important

00:07:46.889 --> 00:07:51.529
to not lose sight of of what you love but
I get it it's you you have to pay your bills

00:07:51.529 --> 00:07:55.069
and it's and it's tough and and sometimes
people I know a lot of people work jobs they

00:07:55.069 --> 00:08:02.120
don't want to work and we've all been there
so was it also helpful with the rental house

00:08:02.120 --> 00:08:07.229
that if something went wrong with the equipment
you could kind of file that away and say okay

00:08:07.229 --> 00:08:11.659
next time I need to yeah just I remember this
guy brought this back because this happened

00:08:11.659 --> 00:08:16.629
or oh yeah definitely I mean when you work
in a roundhouse there are you learn so much

00:08:16.629 --> 00:08:24.169
about the technology and and these things
are it is a technical process and and there

00:08:24.169 --> 00:08:29.619
are people there that are just these these
gurus who know the ins and outs of every little

00:08:29.619 --> 00:08:34.470
thing and and I was very fortunate to be able
to work under them and learn the ins and outs

00:08:34.470 --> 00:08:40.590
of tons of different camera systems and and
as well as I grew up on film so I learned

00:08:40.590 --> 00:08:45.840
a lot of the majority of my camera knowledge
was film knowledge because I learned sort

00:08:45.840 --> 00:08:53.500
of that film was kind of the way to go and
and and and so that was my base understanding

00:08:53.500 --> 00:08:58.780
so digital cameras was like this completely
different world to me but having spent time

00:08:58.780 --> 00:09:02.340
there and working with them you do learn a
lot and so there was a lot of things where

00:09:02.340 --> 00:09:07.220
you go well this camera doesn't really work
that well in low light or this camera we had

00:09:07.220 --> 00:09:10.320
this problem with with this specific lens
that we were using let's try different lens

00:09:10.320 --> 00:09:14.540
next time and so there's a lot of trouble
it very much allows for for troubleshooting

00:09:14.540 --> 00:09:16.152
and learning and educating you know.

00:09:16.152 --> 00:09:16.166
Question For The Viewers: How was this video
helpful to you?

